-# GDExtension ARCore Plugin
+# ARCore plugin (source)
 
+Source and Gradle build of the Godot 4 Android plugin that brings Google ARCore to the AR experiments (Experiment 2 and 3).
+It is a GDExtension (C++) with a small Kotlin/Java part, based on [tGautot/godot_arcore](https://github.com/tGautot/godot_arcore)
+(a fork of [GodotVR/godot_arcore](https://github.com/GodotVR/godot_arcore)) and extended for these samples.
 
-## Usage
-**Note:** [Android Studio](https://developer.android.com/studio) is the recommended IDE for
-developing Godot Android plugins.
-You can install the latest version from https://developer.android.com/studio.
+> [!NOTE]
+> **You don't need to build it to run the experiments.** The built plugin is checked in as
+> [`../addons/ARCorePlugin/`](../addons/ARCorePlugin/) (`bin/{debug,release}/*.aar` plus the GDScript wrapper) and the
+> experiments link it via `bootstrap.ps1`. Build it only after changing the C++, Kotlin or wrapper code.
 
-Clone the repository to your local machine, run the following command in the project root
-directory to initialize the `godot-cpp` submodule:
-```
-git submodule update --init
-```
+## What it provides
 
-### Prerequisites
+The native class `ARCoreInterface` is an `XRInterface` (named "ARCore" in the `XRServer`). The autoload
+`ARCoreInterfaceInstance` ([`ARCoreInterface.gd`](plugin/export_scripts_template/ARCoreInterface.gd)) creates and
+registers it on Android and wraps the native calls:
 
-- OpenJDK: NEEDS version 17
-- Gradle: 8.9 (NEEDS pre 9.0)
-- Make sure the ANDROID_HOME env var is set (usually /home/<username>/Android/Sdk). NOTE: don't use tilde path ("~/Android/Sdk") as it may fail the python `os.path.exists` call
-- Make sure that $ANDROID_HOME/platform-tools is in your PATH variable (and that platform tools are installed in Android Studio)
-- Make sure that the NDKROOT env var is set (should be $ANDROID_HOME/ndk/<version>)
-- Use Ndk versions 25.1.8937393 (in your path above), and also download version 23.2.8568313
+| Feature | API (on `ARCoreInterfaceInstance`) |
+|---|---|
+| Camera feed as background, device pose | `start()`, `get_tracking_status()`, `get_camera_feed()`, `get_camera_rgba_texture()` |
+| ARCore's real projection (for picking that matches the image) | `get_projection_matrix()`, `get_camera_fov()` |
+| Image tracking | `image_tracker_database_add_image()`, `image_tracker_get_tracked_transform()`, … |
+| QR codes (Google ML Kit) | `qr_scanner_start()`, `qr_scanner_set_scan_interval()` (scans run off the render thread), signals `qr_code_found` / `qr_code_lost`, `qr_scanner_get_detected_bounds()` (camera-image pixels), `qr_scanner_get_detected_screen_bounds()` (screen pixels, for overlays) |
+| Planes, point cloud, depth, light estimation | `enable_*()`, `plane_tracker_*()`, `point_cloud_get_points()` |
+| Hit tests | `raycast()`, `screenRayCast()` |
+| Freeze ("inspect mode") | `set_ar_frozen()`: holds pose and image while ARCore keeps tracking |
 
-You are now ready to build the bindings :)
+The higher-level nodes the experiments use (AR rig, image/QR tracked components, inspect camera) are in
+[`../addons/sample_framework_ar/`](../addons/sample_framework_ar/).
 
+## Layout
 
+| Path | What it is |
+|---|---|
+| `plugin/src/main/cpp/` | The GDExtension: `ARCoreInterface` (XR interface), background/plane/point-cloud renderers, image tracker, QR scanner |
+| `plugin/src/main/cpp/include/arcore_c_api.h` | Google's ARCore C API header ([see note](plugin/src/main/cpp/include/README.md)) |
+| `plugin/src/main/java/` | Android plugin entry point (`ARCorePlugin.kt`) and the ML Kit bridge (`QRScannerBridge.java`) |
+| `plugin/export_scripts_template/` | **Source** of the addon's GDScript files (`ARCoreInterface.gd`, `export_plugin.gd`, `plugin.cfg`) |
+| `plugin/build.gradle.kts`, `plugin/CMakeLists.txt` | Gradle and CMake build: versions of Android SDK/NDK, ARCore, ML Kit, Godot |
+| `godot-cpp/` | Godot C++ bindings (MIT), a submodule of upstream; our patches pin it to `10.0.0-stable` |
 
-### Building the C++ bindings
-Build the Android C++ bindings using the following commands. To speed up compilation, add `-jN` at
-the end of the SCons command line where `N` is the number of CPU threads you have on your system.
-The example below uses 4 threads.
-```
-cd godot-cpp
-git checkout 4.4
-scons platform=android target=template_debug -j4
-scons platform=android target=template_release -j4
-```
+## Upstream and patches
 
-When the command is completed, you should have static libraries stored in `godot-cpp/bin` that
-will be used for compilation by the plugin.
+This folder is a git submodule of [tGautot/godot_arcore](https://github.com/tGautot/godot_arcore), pinned to one
+upstream commit. Our changes are in [`../arcore_extension_patches/`](../arcore_extension_patches/) as
+`git format-patch` files, which are reviewed like any other file of the samples repository.
 
-### Building the configured Android plugin
-- In a terminal window, navigate to the project's root directory and run the following command:
-```
-./gradlew assemble
-```
-- On successful completion of the build, the output files can be found in
-  [`plugin/demo/addons`](plugin/demo/addons)
+- **Get the source:** `..\arcore_extension_patches\apply.ps1` checks out the pinned commit, applies the patches as
+  commits on the local branch `samples` and checks out godot-cpp. It discards local changes in this folder.
+- **Change the plugin:** edit here and commit in this folder (a new commit, or `git commit --amend` of the last one).
+  Then run `..\arcore_extension_patches\export.ps1` and commit the changed `*.patch` files in the samples repository.
+  Don't commit the submodule pointer itself: it stays on the upstream commit.
 
-### Building the configured Android plugin with Android Studio
-- Open Android Studio and Click on `Open...` navigate to the git repo and click on `godot_arcore_plugin` where the android robot shows up and wait for Android Studio to finish loading
+## Prerequisites
 
-- In the top bar click on `Add Configuration...` and then `Edit Configurations...`
+- [Android Studio](https://developer.android.com/studio) or the Android command-line tools, with `ANDROID_HOME` set to the
+  SDK folder (use an absolute path, not `~`).
+- From the SDK Manager: Android SDK Platform 36, Build-Tools 36.1.0, CMake 3.22.1, NDK 29.0.14206865 (used by the plugin,
+  see `plugin/build.gradle.kts`) and NDK 28.1.13356709 (the default of godot-cpp; pass `ndk_version=...` to SCons to
+  use another one).
+- JDK 17.
+- Python 3 with [SCons](https://scons.org/) (`pip install scons`) for godot-cpp.
+- Gradle comes with the wrapper (`gradlew`, Gradle 8.9). Don't use a system Gradle 9.
 
-- For the development you can create two configurations, one for building the project and one for cleaning the output folders:
+## Build
 
-- Click on the `+` sign and choose "Gradle" as the type for the configuration
+Run these from this folder (`shared/arcore_extension`).
 
-- In the window to the right give the configuration a name - *`build` in this case* - and under "Run" type `assemble`, which corresponds to the command `./gradlew assemble` which you would do in the command line
+1. Build the godot-cpp static libraries once (they end up in `godot-cpp/bin/`, which CMake links):
 
